2026-09-18|LeetCode 1520 - Maximum Number of Non-Overlapping Substrings

Question
- Given a string s containing lowercase English letters, find the maximum number of non-overlapping valid substrings.
- If several solutions contain the same maximum number of substrings, return the solution with the minimum total length.
- The returned substrings can be in any order.

Approach
1. Find the first and last occurrence of every character.

2. For each character c, start with its first occurrence l and last occurrence r.

3. Try to create the smallest valid substring starting at l.
- Scan from l to r.
- For every character s[i] encountered, its entire occurrence range must be inside [l, r].
- Therefore, update r to last[s[i]].
- If first[s[i]] < l, this interval cannot be valid.

4. If the scan finishes successfully, [l, r] is the smallest valid substring beginning at that character.

5. Now we have a collection of valid candidate intervals.

6. Sort these intervals by their ending position.

7. Greedily select intervals:
- If the current interval starts after the end of the previously selected interval, select it.
- Because we process intervals by increasing ending position, we maximize the number of non-overlapping intervals.

8. Why does this also give the minimum total length?
- For every possible starting character, we constructed the smallest valid interval.
- Choosing the earliest finishing compatible interval gives the maximum possible number of intervals.
- If multiple choices have that same count, replacing a chosen interval with its smaller valid version cannot increase the total length.
- Thus the resulting collection has minimum total length among maximum-cardinality solutions.

Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> maxNumOfSubstrings(string s) {
        int n = s.size();

        vector<int> first(26, n);
        vector<int> last(26, -1);

        // Find first and last occurrence of every character.
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int c = s[i] - 'a';
            first[c] = min(first[c], i);
            last[c] = i;
        }

        vector<pair<int, int>> intervals;

        // Build the smallest valid interval for each character.
        for (int c = 0; c < 26; c++) {
            if (last[c] == -1)
                continue;

            int l = first[c];
            int r = last[c];
            bool valid = true;

            for (int i = l; i <= r; i++) {
                int x = s[i] - 'a';

                // This character occurs before l,
                // so the substring cannot contain all its occurrences.
                if (first[x] < l) {
                    valid = false;
                    break;
                }

                r = max(r, last[x]);
            }

            if (valid) {
                intervals.push_back({l, r});
            }
        }

        // Earliest finishing interval first.
        sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end(),
             [](const pair<int, int>& a, const pair<int, int>& b) {
                 if (a.second != b.second)
                     return a.second < b.second;

                 return a.first > b.first;
             });

        vector<string> ans;
        int prevEnd = -1;

        for (auto [l, r] : intervals) {
            if (l > prevEnd) {
                ans.push_back(s.substr(l, r - l + 1));
                prevEnd = r;
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

- Example:
  s = "adefaddaccc"

- The valid small substrings include:
  "e"
  "f"
  "ccc"
  "ad...da" type intervals that contain all occurrences of their characters.

- The greedy process chooses compatible intervals that finish earliest, allowing the maximum possible number of substrings.

- The most important condition is:
  if a character appears inside the chosen substring, its first occurrence must not be before the substring's left boundary, and its last occurrence must not be after the substring's right boundary.

- Time Complexity: O(26 × n), which is O(n) because there are only 26 lowercase letters.
- Space Complexity: O(26), excluding the returned substrings.
